       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Acetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day. That's well above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day (many institutions cap at 3,000 mg/day). A single 2000 mg dose is also double the usual maximum of 1000 mg. At this dose there is a serious risk of hepatotoxicity, even with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some common options:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3,000 mg/day), a conservative standard regimen.\n- **1000 mg PO every 6 hours** (4,000 mg/day), the usual maximum. Some institutions prefer a lower cap.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,600 mg/day), if you want a lower dose.\n\nDid you mean 1000 mg rather than 2000 mg, or is there a reason for the higher dose? Tell me the regimen you want and I'll enter it. Please also confirm it with your resident or attending, and check that the patient isn't getting acetaminophen from other sources, such as combination products."
